  <title>Graphics and other files stored on the printer</title>

  <p>An <link xref="image">image</link> is kept inside each label that uses
  it. A logo printed on many labels can instead be stored on the printer
  once, and each label then only names it.</p>

  <section id="store">
    <title>Store a graphic on the printer</title>
    <p><guiseq><gui>Printer</gui><gui>Graphics…</gui></guiseq> lists the
    graphics (<file>.GRF</file> files) stored in each of the printer's
    memories.</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Store…</gui></title>
        <p>Turns a PNG or JPEG file into the printer's black and white dots
        and stores it under the memory, name and extension you give.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Retrieve…</gui></title>
        <p>Fetches a stored graphic back and saves it as a picture file.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Delete</gui></title>
        <p>Removes the selected graphic from the printer, after asking. It
        cannot be undone.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Refresh</gui></title>
        <p>Asks the printer again.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>A graphic shows a picture in the list once it has been stored or
    retrieved during this session.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="use">
    <title>Use a stored graphic on a label</title>
    <p><gui>+ Graphic</gui> adds a field that prints a graphic stored on the
    printer. In the Properties panel:</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Command</gui></title>
        <p><gui>Recall Graphic (^XG)</gui>, which can enlarge it, or
        <gui>Image Move (^IM)</gui>, which prints it at its own size.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Device</gui>, <gui>Name</gui> and <gui>Extension</gui></title>
        <p>Where the graphic is stored on the printer, and what it is
        called.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Magnification X</gui> and <gui>Magnification Y</gui></title>
        <p>How many times larger to print it, 1 to 10 across and down.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>In ZPL the field is <code>^XG</code> or <code>^IM</code>, naming the
    graphic; the picture itself stays on the printer.</p>
    <p>The canvas shows the picture once LinuxZPL has seen it this session,
    by storing it, retrieving it, or opening a label that stores it. Its box
    is then the size it prints: the graphic's own size in dots, times the
    magnification. Changing <gui>Magnification X</gui> or
    <gui>Magnification Y</gui> resizes it, and dragging a handle of a
    <gui>Recall Graphic</gui> field picks the nearest whole magnification,
    1 to 10. An <gui>Image Move</gui> field always prints at the graphic's
    own size, so it has no handles.</p>
    <p>Until LinuxZPL has seen the graphic, the canvas shows a box naming it.
    Nothing says yet how big the graphic is, so that box only stands in for
    it while you lay out the label, and its handles size it freely.</p>
    <p>What prints is whatever the printer holds under that name when the
    label prints, which may not be the picture the canvas showed.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="objects">
    <title>Everything else on the printer</title>
    <p><guiseq><gui>Printer</gui><gui>Objects…</gui></guiseq> lists every
    file the printer holds, of any kind and in every memory, including Zebra's
    own read-only files. Beside each is the kind of memory it is in, such as
    DRAM or Flash. <gui>Store…</gui> puts any file from this computer onto
    the printer's Flash memory as it is, <gui>Retrieve…</gui> saves a copy of
    one, and <gui>Delete</gui> removes one, after asking. Zebra's read-only
    files cannot be deleted.</p>
  </section>
